  "id": "DrugBank_DB09287 - MONDO_948",
  "verbalization": "Polyethylene Glycol (PEG) is implicated in the pathogenesis of Xerophthalmia through a chain of indications that link it to Dry Eye Syndrome, a condition that ultimately contributes to Xerophthalmia. The evidence suggests that PEG is associated with Dry Eye Syndrome, which is indicated by its relationship with Lifitegrast and Propylene Glycol, both of which are linked to Xerophthalmia. This implies that PEG may exert a role in the development of Dry Eye Syndrome, which in turn could contribute to Xerophthalmia through mechanisms involving ocular surface dysfunction. The overlapping indications across the paths highlight a potential mechanism where PEG's effects on the ocular surface lead to Dry Eye Syndrome, which may exacerbate or contribute to Xerophthalmia. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of Dry Eye Syndrome as a mediator in the relationship between PEG and Xerophthalmia, suggesting a possible role for PEG in the pathophysiology of this condition.",
